From cc5b161f8e7622a37b5b8143aca825d5e11aa859 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julian Cooper <115541810+Juliansgith@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 23:19:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 42/52] Steering collision prediction: compute a pair's
 orientation terms once

PredictCollisionForSteerings (0x00597800) walks the path nodes of two units,
one in three, and asks UnitsWillCollide (0x00596930) at every step whether
their braking-inflated boxes meet. Each call builds both 2-D boxes from the
units' orientations - two normalised axes per unit (BuildCollisionObb2D) -
and OBB2DIntersects normalises each of the four axes again and projects both
boxes onto it. Nothing in the loop turns either unit, so all of that is the
same at every step; only the centres (position plus lead) and the forward
extents change.

LazyObbPair now keeps those orientation-only terms for the pair - both
frames, and per separating axis its direction and the four |dot| factors -
and computes each part the first time a test needs it (the frames when a step
first gets past the distance precheck, an axis when the separating-axis test
first reaches it), so a pair never costs more than one UnitsWillCollide call
did and repeated steps reuse it. UnitsWillCollidePrepared then does the rest
of UnitsWillCollide with the same operations on the same values, in the same
axis order with the same early outs. UnitsWillCollide itself is unchanged for
its other caller.

In teacher-driven games the steering collision checks were the largest cost
of the sim thread once the swept-path fix was in (about a third of it at
minute 15, the prediction half of that). Per-beat sim checksums of the
30-minute all-AI game identical to the unchanged build's over all 18002 beats.

